    "security_comments":  [
                              {
                                  "id":  7165956390,
                                  "author":  "m-shaheer-shoaib",
                                  "created_at":  "2026-11-25T14:52:44Z",
                                  "updated_at":  "2026-11-25T14:52:44Z",
                                  "html_url":  "https://github.com/i243086-dotcom/Medihub-SQE/pull/11#issuecomment-7165956390",
                                  "body":  "I reviewed the S4502 investigation, JWT configuration in backend/app.py, role-based authorization and the recorded authentication tests.\n\nMediHub configures JWT authentication through the Authorization: Bearer header using JWT_TOKEN_LOCATION = [\u0027headers\u0027], rather than authentication cookies.\n\nThe test evidence covers all 14 protected write routes. Cookie-only requests returned HTTP 401, while requests with a valid cookie but an incorrect-role Authorization header returned HTTP 403. The rejected requests did not modify appointment records.\n\nBased on the source code and recorded test results, I agree that S4502 appears to be a *false positive for the current header-based authentication configuration*, because browsers do not automatically attach these bearer headers as ambient authentication credentials.\n\nI support a documented false-positive disposition for this specific configuration, provided the deployed authentication settings match the tested implementation. Any future cookie-based authentication would require another CSRF assessment."
                              },
                              {
                                  "id":  7166317600,
                                  "author":  "mominkhawaja-codes",
                                  "created_at":  "2026-11-25T15:06:39Z",
                                  "updated_at":  "2026-11-25T15:06:39Z",
                                  "html_url":  "https://github.com/i243086-dotcom/Medihub-SQE/pull/11#issuecomment-7166317600",
                                  "body":  "Security Review — S4502 Authentication and CSRF Assessment\r\n\r\nI reviewed the S4502 technical investigation, public authentication endpoints, frontend token handling and recorded CORS test results.\r\n\r\nThe login endpoint returns the JWT in the JSON response without setting an authentication cookie. The frontend stores the token in `localStorage` and explicitly includes it in the Authorization header for API requests.\r\n\r\nThe registration endpoint does not automatically authenticate the user, and logout removes the client-side token. The recorded cross-origin preflight tests also showed no permissive `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` or credential-sharing headers.\r\n\r\nThese findings support the conclusion that protected write operations do not rely on browser-managed authentication cookies in the tested build.\r\n\r\nTherefore, I agree that S4502 can reasonably be classified as a false positive for the current application configuration, subject to confirmation that no deployment proxy introduces cookie authentication or changes the CORS policy.\r\n\r\nMy assessment is based on the reviewed implementation and recorded tests. I recommend retaining this explanation with the SonarQube finding for future verification."
                              }
                          ],
